<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  Tracked skid-steer ground rover, driven by ArduPilot ArduRover.

  PORTED FROM GAZEBO SIM (gz-sim) TO GAZEBO CLASSIC. The upstream model this came
  from is SDF 1.9 for Harmonic/Garden and will not load here at all. What
  changed, and why each change is not optional:

    - gz-sim system plugins removed entirely. JointStatePublisher,
      OdometryPublisher and the four ApplyJointForce systems have no Classic
      equivalent and no Classic consumer. ApplyJointForce existed only because
      gz-sim's ArduPilotPlugin drives joints through a component; the Classic
      plugin calls Joint::SetForce directly, so the joints move with nothing
      declared.
    - ArduPilotPlugin -> libArduPilotPlugin.so, with Classic's element names
      (listen_addr / fdm_port_in / fdm_port_out, worldFrame, lock_step).
    - <servo_min>/<servo_max> deleted. They are a gz-sim-only feature: the
      Classic FDM packet carries servo output ALREADY normalised to 0..1, so the
      plugin computes cmd = multiplier * (raw + offset) straight from that.
      Leaving them in would be silently ignored, not an error.
    - navsat sensor -> Classic type="gps".
    - The IMU's 180 deg sensor roll is KEPT, alongside
      modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown. An earlier version of this port removed it
      on the theory that Classic flips the body frame only once. That was wrong,
      it shipped, and it reported a level rover as inverted — see the long note on
      the sensor itself for why both rotations are needed.
    - <pose degrees="true"> is SDF 1.9 only; angles here are radians.
    - The front camera moved into its own nested model, rover_front_camera.
      That is not cosmetic — see the note on the include below.

  Geometry, mass and the skid-steer channel mapping are unchanged from upstream:
  bounding box 0.74 x 0.64 x 0.57 m, 60.8 kg, 0.60 m/s top speed. Those three
  numbers are load-bearing — the drive gain, the PID torque clamp and
  rover.parm's speed profile were all sized from them, so changing one means
  resizing the others.

  TRACKS ARE VISUAL, PHYSICS IS FOUR WHEELS. Each side shows a belt plate and a
  ring of tread lugs, but every contact with the terrain is still one of the four
  cylinder collisions — there is not a single collision shape in the track. The
  lugs around a wheel are parented to that wheel's link, so the wheel joint turns
  them and the tread visibly rotates with no plugin and no animated texture; the
  lugs on the straight runs belong to the chassis and therefore do not scroll.
  Lug pitch (6.6 cm) is computed from the lug ring radius so the runs and the
  wheels line up; change one lug dimension and they stop matching.

  True track physics would mean Gazebo's TrackedVehicle / TrackController
  systems, which drive the vehicle themselves and would fight ArduPilotPlugin
  for the joints. Not worth it: ArduRover's skid steer already maps cleanly onto
  wheel velocity, and that is what the whole control chain here is built on.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="rover_core">

    <static>0</static>

    <!-- Front camera as a NESTED model, not a sensor on base_link. terrain/
         make_world.py allocates this asset's MJPEG port by string-matching the
         streaming plugin's name in a model's SDF and then rewriting that file —
         and one of those rewrites replaces EVERY <always_on> in it. With the
         camera in this file, CAMERAS=off would also switch off the IMU below,
         and the rover would lose its only attitude source with nothing in the
         log to say so. iris_with_ardupilot keeps its camera in gimbal_small_2d
         for the same reason.

         The match is on plugin name alone, so do not write that identifier into
         this file even inside a comment — it is spelled out in
         rover_front_camera/model.sdf, which is safe to match, and this file must
         stay unmatched.

         Pose is relative to base_link's frame: level, at the front of the
         sensor head. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://rover_front_camera</uri>
      <pose>0.26 0 0.52 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>
    <joint name="front_camera_mount" type="fixed">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>rover_front_camera::base_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- ================= CHASSIS ================= -->
    <link name="base_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0.28 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>54.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>2.011</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>2.754</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>3.177</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <!-- Body collision sits ABOVE the wheels on purpose: the wheels are the
           only things that should touch the terrain. A body box reaching to the
           ground would catch on the heightmap and stop the rover dead. -->
      <collision name="body_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.33 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.66 0.58 0.30</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Every visual below names a material SCRIPT. Inline <ambient>/<diffuse>
           is not an option, however reasonable it looks: gzweb takes colour only
           from material.script.name (gz3d/src/gziface.js parseMaterial), so a
           visual carrying just RGB values resolves to nothing and renders plain
           white in the browser while still looking correct to the server-side
           camera sensors. This model shipped that way once and appeared as a
           white blob. The colours themselves are in
           materials/scripts/rover.material — repaint there, not here. -->
      <!-- lower drive housing between the tracks -->
      <visual name="housing_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.14 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.58 0.36 0.12</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Housing</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- main upper body -->
      <visual name="body_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.34 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.64 0.46 0.28</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Body</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- sensor head (cameras + RTK), at the front top -->
      <visual name="head_visual">
        <pose>0.16 0 0.52 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.20 0.30 0.10</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Head</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- left track. The belt plate spans the two road wheels; the
           straight runs carry the same lug boxes at the same 6.6 cm pitch as
           the wheels, so tread is continuous all the way round. These run
           lugs are on the chassis, so they do NOT scroll — only the wheel
           lugs turn. At the ranges this sim is flown from that reads as a
           moving track; making the runs scroll too needs a plugin stepping
           lug poses, or an Ogre scroll_anim texture that gzweb would not
           animate. Visual only: physics is still the four wheel cylinders. -->
      <visual name="left_belt">
        <pose>0 0.2500 0.1200 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.4400 0.1120 0.2100</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Track</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="left_lug_bot_00">
        <pose>-0.1979 0.2500 -0.0060 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.0420 0.1250 0.0180</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Lug</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="left_lug_top_00">
        <pose>-0.1979 0.2500 0.2460 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.0420 0.1250 0.0180</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <uri>model://rover_core/materials/scripts/rover.material</uri>
            <name>Rover/Lug</name>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>
